# modules-kernel/generic
#
# Empty MODULE overlay — the DEFAULT device class. Applied on top of the base
# kernel/config:
#     scripts/kconfig/merge_config.sh -m .config kernel/config modules-kernel/generic
#     make olddefconfig
#
# This file is NOT a full .config. It lists ONLY options that are specific to
# the generic device class — and the generic class adds NONE. The base
# kernel/config already carries the filesystems, virtio, serial console and
# the disabled subsystems shared across every device, so the generic overlay
# needs no CONFIG_ options of its own. It exists so the bootc-debs-builder
# pipeline can produce a real `linux-modules-generic_<ver>_<arch>.deb` (an
# empty package that satisfies the `linux-modules-${DEVICE}` dependency in the
# Containerfile) instead of relying on apt silently ignoring an empty package
# name. The modules built from this overlay are OPTIONAL — the base image
# (linux-image-common) works without them.
#
# Device class: generic (no SoC, no device-specific drivers). The default
# DEVICE value in Containerfile.minimal; resolves to a harmless empty
# meta-package so the apt install line always names a real package.
#
# Conventions:
#   # reason in English
#   CONFIG_OPTION=y           # built-in
#   CONFIG_OPTION=m           # module
#   # CONFIG_OPTION is not set  # disabled

# =============================================================================
# No device-specific options. The generic overlay intentionally carries zero
# CONFIG_ directives — every option shared across all devices lives in
# kernel/config. This file is a header-only stub so the builder produces a
# valid (empty) linux-modules-generic .deb.
# =============================================================================